from pathlib import Path
from shutil import copyfile
from docx import Document
P=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;f=P.parent/'redshift_paper/temporal_redshift_paper.docx'
d=Document(f);assert not any(x.text.startswith('Appendix F.') for x in d.paragraphs)
copyfile(f,P/'paper_before.docx');old=[x._p.xml for x in d.paragraphs]
def p(t,s='Normal'):d.add_paragraph(t,s)
def h(t):p(t,'Heading 2')
def e(t):p(t,'Equation')
d.add_page_break()
p('Appendix F. Shared wave propagation, curvature and rotating geometry','Heading 1')
p('The next candidate applies the same temporal propagation rule to light and gravitational waves. Both signs of spatial curvature are retained, with rotation treated as an additional geometric property. Atomic invariance remains a requirement to derive. The earlier unequal-wave-speed result in E.2 is preserved as a limitation of that specific action, not a universal objection to time-based redshift.')
h('F.1 Common propagation for both wave species')
e('c_light(t,x) = c_GW(t,x) = c₀/n(t,x).')
p('This rule requires the same characteristic metric and null paths, including directional and gravitational delays. Equal present speeds alone are insufficient. In geometric optics, a quadratic wave action with kinetic coefficient A_X>0 and gradient coefficient A_X c₀²/n² gives this speed for either transverse electromagnetic modes or tensor modes. Equality of those principal propagation operators removes the enormous differential travel delay by construction for simultaneous emission along the same path. Emission-time differences can still produce a small arrival offset such as that associated with GW170817 [14].')
p('This is an explicit candidate requirement, not a completed nonlinear gravitational action. Changing the tensor kinetic operator also changes gravitational dynamics, constraints and energy exchange. Amplitudes and gravitational-wave luminosity distances need not match electromagnetic ones merely because their paths coincide. The atomic and static-background problems in E.1–E.2 must therefore be reconsidered in the new action rather than declared solved.')
h('F.2 First comparison of both curvature signs')
p('Retain R=ln(1+zHD)/κ and the fixed Cepheid calibration from Appendix E. For constant-curvature isotropic space, with curvature radius R_c, replace the Euclidean area distance by:')
e('D_L = (1+zHEL) R_c sin(R/R_c)    [positive curvature],')
e('D_L = (1+zHEL) R_c sinh(R/R_c)   [negative curvature].')
p('Both approach the original flat law as R_c tends to infinity. Positive curvature is restricted to paths before the first conjugate point; this calculation includes no multiple wraps or images. Each branch scans one radius against the already-inspected 960 supernovae, preserving κ, absolute magnitude and the full covariance. This is an exploratory extension, not a held-out test.')
p('Positive curvature prefers the flat boundary, χ²=898.475. Negative curvature prefers R_c≈4547 Mpc and gives χ²=873.041, an improvement of 25.433 with one additional fitted parameter. Its four mean magnitude residuals become approximately +0.129, +0.172, +0.139 and +0.163 using the earlier bin weights. It improves the trend but leaves an offset and remains worse than the fixed reference’s χ²=838.483. This geometric fit does not establish a static gravitational solution or determine cosmic rotation.')

d.add_page_break()
h('F.3 A Gödel-style branch')
p('Gödel’s original solution is a homogeneous, rotating, nonexpanding spacetime [25]. Technically it is stationary rather than static: rotation prevents a global slicing orthogonal to the rotating matter congruence. It also contains closed timelike curves. A Gödel-type extension can retain the nonexpansion and rotation as hypotheses without automatically importing every property of the original solution; any causal restriction or modified dynamics must be specified.')
p('Rotation is not the same variable as positive or negative spatial curvature. A rotating candidate generally needs direction-dependent null-ray and beam-area calculations rather than simply substituting a sine or hyperbolic sine. The preceding radius fits therefore compare nonrotating geometries and do not select a Gödel solution. Pure stationary Gödel geometry also does not itself supply the assumed exponential temporal redshift; an evolving propagation field is still an additional hypothesis.')
p('There is a concrete compatibility condition for the timing field. A nonzero gradient aligned everywhere with the matter four-velocity, ∇n=f u, would make that congruence hypersurface-orthogonal and hence irrotational. It cannot simultaneously describe nonzero Gödel-type vorticity. Our earlier action used an independent vector, so alignment is not mandatory, but the new model must specify how the evolving n field relates to rotating observers. The original Gödel spacetime has no global time function suitable for an unrestricted n(t) history; a causal Gödel-type branch, a domain of applicability or different boundary conditions are needed.')
h('F.4 What the CMB can discriminate')
p('The CMB is useful without assuming a Big Bang, but its mean temperature alone does not choose curvature or rotation. A model must specify the origin and spatial distribution of its radiation, how a thermal spectrum is established, and how photons and polarization propagate through the proposed geometry. For an initially uniform thermal bath with adiabatic frequency rescaling, directional stretching gives δT/T≈−δ ln S. Unknown source-temperature variations add a separate term. This relation connects a predicted directional timing pattern to an observable; it is not yet a prediction of the pattern itself.')
p('Temperature maps test directional structure and angular correlations. Polarization tests additional patterns from scattering and transport, including any model-predicted handedness or mixing of polarization modes. The complete spectrum and lensing information provide further checks. The acoustic-peak angular positions cannot be turned into a model-independent curvature estimate by borrowing the standard early-universe sound horizon: this universe must derive its own physical correlation scale and radiation history.')
p('Published Planck temperature-plus-polarization analyses strongly constrain specified Bianchi models [26], but their expansion-normalized parameters and radiation histories cannot simply be applied to a nonexpanding Gödel-type model. Rotation also does not guarantee a unique visible spiral: shear-free rotating examples with isotropic background radiation exist in other theories [27]. Their existence is a caution about inference, not validation of this candidate.')

d.add_page_break()
h('F.5 A CMB test that can distinguish the candidates')
p('Define a small family of geometries with fixed spatial size: positive curvature, negative curvature, and explicitly rotating variants. For each, specify a consistent shared photon/tensor characteristic metric, a radiation-source model, and a timing-field history. Fit only parameters that have been defined in advance, such as curvature radius, rotation magnitude and axis. The nonrotating curvature fit in F.2 is a preliminary brightness diagnostic; the rotating model and CMB predictions have not yet been computed.')
p('Ray tracing must predict beam areas, frequency shifts and directional arrival-time effects. Polarization transport and the chosen scattering history must then produce temperature and polarization maps or their statistical distributions. If arbitrary initial sky patterns are freely adjusted to reproduce every observed feature, the geometry has not been tested. Initial correlations and nuisance parameters must therefore be constrained and counted in model comparisons.')
p('The existing binned TT and EE data in Appendix C cannot test quadrupole–octopole alignment: they start near multipole 48 and discard orientation. The next observational inputs are low-multipole temperature and polarization maps, masks, beam models and noise simulations. Frequency and component-separation comparisons test foreground sensitivity, but maps derived from the same observations are not independent confirmations. Parameters selected from temperature should generate a stated prediction for polarization, with shared correlations handled in the likelihood.')
p('The assumed “axis of evil” in the fictional universe is treated as an alignment to explain, not as proof of rotation. A candidate must predict more than an axis chosen after looking at the map: for example a linked angular pattern, polarization signal or direction-dependent brightness residual. Axis selection, masks and the number of patterns searched must enter simulations of chance alignments. Planck reports several large-angle temperature features but no unambiguous corresponding polarization detections [12].')
p('The working conclusion is therefore conditional. Shared propagation removes the old differential-wave-delay problem at the level of the new rule. Negative curvature currently improves the fixed-coefficient supernova comparison more than positive curvature. A Gödel-style rotating branch remains a distinct possibility whose timing-field compatibility, radiation predictions and gravitational dynamics require derivation. No CMB likelihood, rotation fit or stable nonexpanding background solution is claimed in this extension.')
h('Additional references and reproducibility for Appendix F')
p('[25] Gödel, K. An Example of a New Type of Cosmological Solutions of Einstein’s Field Equations of Gravitation. Rev. Mod. Phys. 21, 447 (1949). https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.21.447','Reference')
p('[26] Saadeh, D., et al. How Isotropic is the Universe? Phys. Rev. Lett. 117, 131302 (2016). https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.07178','Reference')
p('[27] Obukhov, Y. N., Chrobok, T., and Scherfner, M. Rotation in string cosmology (2003). https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0302027','Reference')
p('Companion: shared_curved_update.zip contains curvature_test.py, curvature_results.json, a reproduction note and the Appendix E data dependencies. The scalar scan includes the flat boundary and checks a coarse grid. κ and the Cepheid calibration are unchanged; one curvature radius is fitted per branch.','Reference')
assert [x._p.xml for x in d.paragraphs[:len(old)]]==old
d.save(f);print('Preserved original paragraphs:',len(old),'Total:',len(d.paragraphs))
